    "content_en": "I used to wonder: \"How can I actually start earning money from my coding skills?\" Seeing developers on social media sharing massive revenue figures made it feel exciting, yet confusing when you're just starting out as an IT student.\n\nHere is the step-by-step roadmap I used to land my very first paying web development clients, along with the mistakes you should avoid.\n\n## 1. Choose a Specific Niche\n\nDon't market yourself as a \"full-stack developer who does everything\". Choose a focused niche:\n- High-converting landing pages for local businesses\n- Company profile websites for consulting firms and clinics\n- Custom booking or catalog websites for regional tourism operators\n\n## 2. Build 2–3 Realistic Portfolio Projects\n\nEven before you have paying clients, build 2–3 polished, complete web projects. Treat them like real client briefs:\n- Give them realistic business branding and typography\n- Implement responsive mobile designs and smooth interactions\n- Deploy them live on free platforms like Vercel or GitHub Pages with custom domains or clean URLs\n\n## 3. Where to Find Your First Client\n\nYour first client will almost never come from international bidding platforms. Look at your immediate surroundings:\n- Local businesses, cafes, or family friends with outdated or missing websites\n- Campus student organizations and local community groups\n- Running low-budget Meta Ads targeted at business owners in your city\n\n## 4. How Much to Charge? Never Work for Free!\n\nFor my first project, I charged between IDR 500,000 to IDR 2,000,000. While modest, getting paid creates mutual commitment and professional accountability.\n\n- **Rule 1**: Never build a commercial project for free. Even a small fee establishes respect.\n- **Rule 2**: Always request a 50% upfront down payment before writing a single line of code.\n\n## 5. Professionalism from Day One\n\nThe mistakes I made on early projects taught me valuable lessons:\n- Clarify the project scope in writing before starting to avoid scope creep.\n- Don't ask questions you could easily research yourself.\n- Explain technical updates in simple, client-friendly terms.\n\n> \"Your first client isn't about having 10 years of experience. It's about having the courage to raise your hand and say: 'I can help you build this.'\"\n\n## Roadmap Summary\n\n1. Pick a clear niche and ideal client profile.\n2. Build and deploy 2–3 realistic, high-quality showcase projects.\n3. Reach out to local businesses or run targeted local ads.\n4. Charge a fair price with 50% upfront deposit.\n5. Deliver on time with clean communication and request a client testimonial.",
